# Code owners — GitHub requests a review from the owner of every path a pull
# request touches, automatically.
#
# The ruleset on `main` has "require code owner review" turned on, so these
# lines are not advisory: a pull request cannot merge until the owner of every
# matched path has approved it.
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style, and **the last matching line wins** — which is the
# opposite of how most config files work and the usual way this file goes
# wrong. Keep the catch-all first and narrow rules below it.

# Everything, unless something below claims it.
*                           @heshannethmina

# The concurrent code. Called out separately not because ownership differs
# today, but because a change here needs the race detector run against it and
# a read of whether room state is still owned by a single goroutine. When
# there is a second reviewer, this is the first path to hand over.
/backend/internal/ws/       @heshannethmina

# Schema changes are one-way in a way code is not: a migration that has run in
# production cannot be edited, only followed by another one.
/backend/migrations/        @heshannethmina

# Auth, tokens and the plan/promo logic that decides who gets in and for how
# long.
/backend/internal/auth/     @heshannethmina
/backend/internal/api/      @heshannethmina

# CI, deployment and the repository's own automation. A change here can be
# green and still break production, and it is the one place a pull request can
# alter how pull requests are checked.
/.github/                   @heshannethmina
/render.yaml                @heshannethmina
/docker-compose.yml         @heshannethmina
/docker-compose.app.yml     @heshannethmina

# The design record. Not code, but the reasoning behind most of the decisions
# in it — worth the same review as the code it explains.
/CLAUDE.md                  @heshannethmina
